de director Guillermo del  Toro a household name the film uses dark  fantasy to create a dream world in which  the horrors of fascist Spain are  reflected by the horrors of a big maze  with monsters in it the monster who  gives his name to the title is the faun  but the real nightmare star is the pale  man whose eyeballs in the palms bit is  probably the film's most memorable  visual the actor behind the faun would  probably be mad that the pale man  upstaged him if it weren't for the fact  that they were played by the same actor  Doug Jones Jones is reportedly a very  nice person despite his monstrous roles  so it's no surprise to see him showing  some affection behind the scenes to his  makeup artist 1974 is the Texas Chainsaw  Massacre is one of the most influential  and beloved horror films ever made  1986 is Texas Chainsaw Massacre 2 has  something less of a critical consensus  behind it but his reputation has grown  in recent years director tobe Hooper  wanted the sequel to expand on the black  comedy elements of the first film so it  really ramps up the satire as well as  the gore to make it something almost as  over-the-top as the first film is  understated the spirit of the film  really shows in this behind-the-scenes  photo in which Leatherface is playing  cards with his brothers to cook and chop  top in a game that seems to have gone  somewhat awry but far less awry than  their usual games the Hellraiser  franchise is about demons from another  dimension and leather gear with  elaborate body mods that steal people's  souls easily the most recognizable  character is pinhead a leader of descent  abides portrayed in the first seven  films by Doug Bradley according to an  interview with birth movies death  promoting Hellraiser 3 in 1992 Bradley  was walking through New York and full  costume so that he could present a prop  to Planet Hollywood naturally his  presence drew quite a crowd and at some  point one of the dozens of people  following him down the street asked him  to take a picture holding a baby  Bradley refers to this as pinhead  running for president Wes Craven's and  Nightmare on Elm Street ended up  exploding into a massive franchise and  pretty much everyone agrees that out of  all the sequels the best one is part  three the dream warriors one of the most  important additions to the Elm Street  mythos in this film is the role of  Kristen Parker portrayed by Patricia  Arquette who has the ability to summon  other people into the dream world at  will and one of her most famous scenes  is the one in which Freddy appears as a  giant snake that tries to swallow  Kristen whole until he stabbed with a  shard of glass and pukes are up as cool  as that scene is it's somewhat undercut  by this shot of Arquette not at all  bothered to be halfway down Freddy's  gullet check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos are coming soon subscribe to our  YouTube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

